  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nTraining thunderstorms are currently causing flash flooding\nacross portions of southeast Ohio. These training thunderstorms\nare being caused by mid- level vorticity flowing on the \nnorthern periphery of a large area of high pressure, in\ncombination with a stationary surface boundary. The atmosphere\nremains very moist, with precipitable water values ranging from\n2.00 to 2.25 inches. The main concern through daybreak Monday\nwill be flash flooding across southeast Ohio counties.\n\nThe aforementioned stationary front will lift northeastward \ninto Pennsylvania after daybreak with the entire forecast area\ntransitioning into the warm sector. Additional rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms are expected area-wide Sunday through\nSunday night as additional mid-level waves track across the \nforecast area. It's difficult to pinpoint exactly where the main\nfocus of thunderstorm development will be during this \ntimeframe, as new development will be triggered by thunderstorm \noutflow boundaries from previous complexes. We will be \nmonitoring trends closely. In addition to flooding, severe\nthunderstorms will also be possible Sunday with any\nthunderstorms. 1,500 J/kg of MLCAPE and 30-35 kts of 0-6km bulk\nshear will allow for organized thunderstorm development that\ncould turn severe at times.\n\nA cold front will finally cross the forecast area from northwest\nto southeast Monday afternoon. This will bring high pressure and\nthe return of much-needed drier weather behind it. \n\nThunderstorms will return to the forecast Thursday and Friday as\na trough and another area of low pressure approach from the \nwest.",
